Date: Sun, 12 Sep 93 17:11:24 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: The Evidence of the Senses

Ray Cromwell: 
>Considering that nanotechnology could reduce a 1 teraop computer down
>to a cm cube, I don't see how you could possibly argue "we will never
>have enough CPU". What about a computer more powerful than the human
>brain itself? Upload the brain to that computer and REPROGRAM the 
>consciousness of that person to believe that cheesying looking 
>vector graphics is "reality" and he never knew otherwise.

I, for one, will strongly decline volunteering for this 
experiment.  When I'm uploaded, I'll want to devote as many bit*cycles 
to my own thinking processes as possible.  Rather than concern myself 
with cheesy simulacrums of my former biological life -- food, sex, music, 
etc. -- I'll want to take advantage of the new environment -- vast databases 
to explore, the sensual world of panspectrum, nay panreality scientific
instruments, manipulating a wide variety of industrial and transport
equipment, etc.  I want reality bandwidth amplification, not reality 
simulation.

I might simulate some aspects of the old narrow human
sensuality in order to avoid a psyche-destroying abrupt transition, 
but both well before and after the uploading I will undertake a gradual, 
balanced, planned obsolecence of that which is irrelevent and trivial 
to a successful upload.  If I stop believing that the simulation is fake 
and trivial, that will be my undoing.  Those who devote more bit*cycles 
to simulating reality than they do to mastering reality, all in order to 
fool themselves, will have rendered themselves irrelevent in the posthuman
world.  If so interested in the human's view of reality, why not just 
stay human?  

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

