From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: The Evidence of the Senses 

Christina M Grimes says:
> Mike Price writes:
> MP> My thanks to Ratha for quoting a long section from _Consciousness
> MP> Explained_, "How are Hallucinations Possible?" by Daniel C.
> MP> Dennett. Dennet's argument amounts to: "I can't imagine how
> MP> realistic VR could work, so it won't, ever" Enough said. 
> MP>     Dennet/Starr/Rand 0, Perry 1
> 
> Pardon my thickheadedness if I fail to see how Dennett's argument
> reduces to that. To me, the argument seemed to be that a completely
> interactive, to the last realistic detail, complete simulation--or, if
> you like, "VR"--would have to reach a combinatorial explosion, that is,
> it would have to simulate much too much simultaneous and instantaneous
> information, conforming to the subject's every action, than any present
> computer could possibly supply.

Ah, but Dennett has never thought about this enough. In fact, you
don't get a combinatorial explosion. There is a FINITE amount that a
human is sensing at any time, and you can use tricks to deal with most
of it. You don't have to simulate the surface feel of a table when
someone isn't touching it, you don't have to simulate the deep
structure of the table unless someone breaks it apart, you don't have
to simulate the lowest level of wood fibers unless they take out a
hand lens, etc. You don't have to do a detailed rendering of what's
behind someone's head. I could go on, but I've made my point.

> If you had a computer of infinite power,
> theoretically you would be able to work it. However, a computer of
> infinite power is pretty hard to come by, and it will take quite a few
> years before we can come up with one.

You are surrounded by a finite world, so you need only finite power. I
could make the theoretical argument that the amount of computation
needed to simulate the input to your senses is strictly bounded by the
number of bits of input your senses take, but producing a rigorous
argument would be hard for me on a lazy Sunday evening. However, you
can see my last post for information on how much computing power will
(conservatively) be available in the next century.

Perry

