Date: Sun, 12 Sep 93 22:30:18 EDT
From: pmetzger@lehman.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: miscalculation, and Jovian Minds

In a recent message, I said, assuming that nanocomputers fitting in
bacteria size volumes could compute 1000 times faster than a cray, a
one meter cube filled with them could compute more in one second than
a cray could in 100,000 years. I was sadly too conservative by a
factor of 1E6, having idiotically squared where I should have cubed.
I also made some other errors.

A one meter cube of computers a micron across that are each 1000 times
faster than a cray (a Comput-A-Cube(TM)) contains ((1E6)^3)*1E3 Cray
equivalents, which is 1E21 cray equivalents. Dividing 1E21 crays by
the number of seconds in a year, 31536000, we get 31709791983764
cray-years/sec, which is close enough to 3.2E13, as a figure for how
many years it would take one cray to catch up with a cube like the one
I envisioned. Since the universe has been around for about 1.5E10
years, thats a cray running for 2113 universe-lifetimes -- to do the
equivalent of ONE SECOND of computation. (Note that as a
universe-lifetime I am only counting the lifespan of the universe thus
far -- who knows how long it will run for...)

Power and cooling problems might make this cube impractical -- but
then again, we have orders of magnitude to spare. We can afford to go
100 or 1000 times slower and the number is still mindboggling.

Dennett says that doing realistic VR is forever beyond our
capabilities. Even using naive techniques, that Comput-A-Cube(TM)
could simulate everything visible to your senses in the room around
you, and still like have times to do thousands, if not millions, of
other people's environments as well. I shudder to think of what it
could do if it was using sophisticated tricks instead of naive
simulation.

This, of course, brings up the interesting question of Jupiter-sized
brains. 

Jupiter has a radius of 70,000 km, 7E7 meters. It has a volume
therefore on the order of magnitude of 1E24 cubic meters. It could
therefore hold 10^45 Cray/Equivalents, or enough to do as much work in
a second as one cray could do in 2E27 universe (thus far) lifetimes.

More realistically, however, there is the fact that brains are more
complicated than crays, so what we really want to know is how much
more powerful a BRAIN that size would be. Given Drexler's estimate of
1cc brains running 1E7 times faster than normal, we can see that a
cubic meter would contain 1E6 such machines, thus yielding 1E13
brain/equivalents per cubic meter, or 1E37 brain/equivalents per
Jupiter. The power consumed by such a setup is left as an exercise to
the reader, as is the method needed to cool it. Please let me know
your ideas on these topics, as I have only a few centuries to work out
the design before I move in.

Perry

