Date: Sun, 12 Sep 93 22:02:55 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: The Evidence of the Senses

Mike Price:

> Dennet's argument amounts to:
> "I can't imagine how realistic VR could work, so it won't, ever"
> Enough said.

I believe that Dennett's point wasn't really to claim that realistic
VR couldn't work with some as-yet undeveloped technology at some
future date.  Dennett is a materialist and is fully aware of the
consequences of that position.  The point he's trying to make, right
at the start of his thoughts on the nature of consciousness, is that,
first -- if we weren't brains in vats yesterday, we're not today
either (because it's unlikely that a powerful enough computer exists
or will exist for a "long time" [a relative term!]); second -- it
seems to him unlikely that the brain's own circuitry could provide
realistic "inward VR" at a level near that of sense data because
it's not powerful enough to do the simulation.  He uses this oddity
(that VR-like "strong" hallucinations seem to happen, sort of,
nevertheless) to open a crack into the machinery that may be at
work in our minds.

By all this I'm just saying that I don't believe Dennett's thought
experiment to be relevant to the discussion.  I haven't been following
it too closely -- but if somebody is really claiming that our senses
reveal an objective reality unerringly, that's clearly hooey.  When two
witnesses of some event see that event differently (as apparently happens
all the time), wouldn't that be a contradiction?

derek


