Date: Sun, 12 Sep 93 23:03:40 EDT
From: pmetzger@lehman.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: more on Jovian Brains

It occured to me that some folks might object that a Jovian Brain
would have too slow a reaction time because of speed of light
considerations. I don't believe such stuff, but for the sake of fun
I'm happy to scale back my calculations slightly.

Assume that nerve signals travel at about the speed of sound --
3E2m/s. The speed of light, 3E8m/s, is almost exactly 1E6 times
faster. A human brain is about .15m on a side, so an object capable of
reacting as fast as a human brain but using speed-'o-lite(TM)
calculations could be 150000m across, or about the size of a good
sized planetoid. Such an object has a volume of around 1E15, so a
superbrain that size could only calculate at 1E28 human brain
equivalents, which still is pretty good, I'd say -- but I'll still go
for developing the architecture required for Jovian brain size since
thats still a million to ten million times better.


Perry

