From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Subject: Jupiter Brain Engineering 101

  I don't buy the idea that a Jovian brain needs super-fast
communication to all parts of itself for fast reaction time. Why not
a hierarchy?  Remember Vinge's space bobble defense systems in Marooned
In Real Time? Perimeter autons with fast flicker time, inner autons with
slower flicker time, and the inner most ship staying bobbled for long
periods of time. How 'bout this design for a Jovian brain.

Outer layer ["reflex layer"] - fast processors and sensors which relay data
to adjacent processors at relativistic speeds. These processors would be
responsible for collection of sensor data and reflex actions in times of
emergency.

Inner preprocessing layer ["cortex"] - data from reflex layer is organized
with other data (taking into account the delays involved in collecting
data from something with the surface area of Jupiter). Latency is probably
on the order of hundreds of microseconds to milliseconds.

The core layer ["the thinking conciousness area"] - here, thoughts
could take on the order of milliseconds to seconds.

  Large jovian brains might be a bad idea anyway. They have poor 
maneuverability, are centralized, and make a good target for attack. If minds
can be distributed, it might be a better idea to put the reflex and cortex
areas on hundreds of small asteroids. The thinking layer would simply be
the connection of all of these objects over long links. Thoughts might
take hours to years, however, fault tolerance, expandability, and 
maneuverability would be greatly increased. (see Vinge's "mailman" in
True Names)

  OORT Cloud sized brain anyone?


-Ray

p.s. as a side benefit, long journeys would pass quicker because of
the subjective time for thoughts to complete. Space exploration would simply
mean sending a part of yourself off on a journey (and out of contact) for
