Date: Mon, 13 Sep 1993 02:27:12 -0400 (EDT)
From: Christina M Grimes <cg39+@andrew.cmu.edu>
Subject: The Evidence of the Senses

I stand corrected. I hadn't considered those two points--1. the
computing explosion due to nanotech and 2. the limited amount of
simulation to be done at any given time--which, combined, might be able
to answer the objection. (My apologies to Mike for the indignant flame.)
Still, I have a nagging feeling that a simulated reality would seem
"canned" to the subject--after all, if given free exploration of the
imaginary world, se should be able to get a pile of maps from a local
library that will give accurate information about all geographical
landmasses, water bodies, etc., access all books and periodicals known
by sem to have been written and to reread them in full (as well as be
able to verify that there are still millions se doesn't know about, and
read any one at will), be able to look up any obscure scientific fact or
any particular paper, be able to fly to India to see the Taj Mahal or
Greece to see the Pantheon, go to a childhood home and check out
nostalgic items known only to sem, etc., etc. My point: That's gotta be
one *hell* of a computer, with one *hell* of a hard drive. Even if the
person had been "raised" in this simulation and whose world was randomly
generated and completely personal, the information that would eventually
have been accumulated about that world (and that is necessary to
continue to give an impression of internally consistent reality) would
reach astronomical levels, if not a combinatorial explosion. 

Question: The power of computers to come has been discussed at length in
order to refute my claim that complete simulation is impossible. What if
someone were to upload into one such computer? What kind of computing
power would it take to perform such a reality simulation on _it_? Would
that require--attotech? 7:) Seriously, would it become in fact
impossible?

What was the original point, anyway? I can't seem to find the first post
in my message list. There have been allusions to Rand's theory of
consciousness that "guarantees that what one experiences is necessarily
reality", but somehow I don't remember the theory that way. My knowledge
of Rand is sketchy, but I seem to remember reasoning that goes like
this: You start with the axiom Existence Exists. If that's false, then
you're fucked at the start. If it's true, then, everything else
necessarily follows and works out. You really can't *prove* that
existence exists, because doubting it is tantamount to questioning the
validity of logic, and once you do that, nothing can be proved at all.
The next step from there is assuming that your senses work. Whether
you're experiencing "reality" or experiencing something simulated and
piped through your nerves, I think, would be beside the point. As long
as you get any coherent picture of reality, you assume your senses are
good messengers. (i.e., you may be colorblind, but your sight still
produces a coherent and internally consistent picture.) Any corrections
on this would be appreciated.

Hey, I've got another question: Could a mind that had been "raised" on
such a simulation be transferred into "reality" and still function
effectively? This question becomes particularly interesting if the
person's world had been radically altered, i.e. the law of
non-contradiction had been done away with, the laws of physics had been
overhauled, etc. Would this produce "reality shock"?

It's late, forgive me for anything overlooked or any major flaws in reasoning.

